    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend chapter 217 of the laws of 2015, amending the education law relating to certified school psychologists and special education services and programs for preschool children with handicapping conditions, in relation to the effectiveness thereof",
    "basePrintNo": "S6851",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S6851-2015",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §3, Chap 217 of 2015",
    "lawSection": "Education",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S6851\n\n TITLE OF BILL :  An act to amend chapter 217 of the laws of 2015,\namending the education law relating to certified school psychologists\nand special education services and programs for preschool children\nwith handicapping conditions, in relation to the effectiveness thereof\n\n PURPOSE :\n\nThis bill would allow approved multidisciplinary evaluation programs\nto employ a certified school psychologist to conduct a\nmultidisciplinary evaluation of a preschool child or an infant or\ntoddler having or suspected of having a disability. This bill would\nexpire on June 30, 2018.\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\n\nSection 1. Amends paragraph (d) of subdivision 6 of 4410 of the\neducation law to allow multidisciplinary evaluation programs approved\npursuant to section 4410 of the education law to employ a certified\nschool psychologist on a full time or part time salary basis, which\nmay include an hourly, weekly or monthly basis or on a fee for\nevaluation services basis provided that the school psychologist is\nemployed by and under the dominion and control of a program that has\nbeen approved pursuant to paragraph b of subdivision nine of this\nsection or subdivision nine-a of this section to conduct a\nmultidisciplinary evaluation of a preschool child having or suspected\nof having a disability.\n\nSection 2. Amends subdivision 1 of section 7605 of the education law\nto exempt from licensure until June 30, 2018, certified school\npsychologists employed on a full time or part time salary basis which\nmay include an hourly, weekly or monthly basis or on a fee for\nevaluation services basis provided that the certified school\npsychologist is employed by and under the dominion and control of a\nprogram that has been approved in accordance with title two-A of\narticle twenty five of the public health law to deliver early\nintervention programs for the purpose of conducting multidisciplinary\nevaluations, service where preschool special education program or\nearly interventions programs services are authorized.\n\nSection 3. Establishes the effective date and repeal date of this law.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\n\nSection 4410 of the education law allows approved center based\nprograms to employ a certified school psychologist in a preschool\nsetting. Recently, the State Education Department issued a special\neducation field advisory and learned that multidisciplinary evaluation\nprograms are also using certified school psychologists to conduct\nevaluations of preschool students with disabilities even though they\nare not permitted under law to do so. In speaking with the field,\nshortages of licensed psychologists have made it difficult for these\nmultidisciplinary evaluation programs to ensure timely psychological\nevaluations of preschool students with disabilities as is required by\nlaw. The federal Individuals with Disabilities Education Act (IDEA)\nrequires that initial evaluations of preschool children with\ndisabilities be conducted within 60 days of parental consent for the\nevaluation. In addition, state law requires that approved\nmultidisciplinary evaluation programs, selected by the parent, conduct\nsuch evaluations. It is important that these evaluations continue to\noccur in a timely manner. This legislation would temporarily allow\nmultidisciplinary evaluation programs to continue to employ a\ncertified school psychologist to conduct these evaluations while this\nissue is investigated further. Also, the bill makes clear that nothing\nin the legislation should be interpreted to authorize a certified\nschool psychologist or group of such psychologists to independently\npractice or form and an independent group.\n\n LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n\nNew bill.\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\n\nTBD.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nThis act shall take effect immediately and shall be deemed to have\nbeen in full force and effect on and after July 1, 2014, provided,\nhowever, that the provisions of this act shall expire and be deemed\nrepealed June 30, 2018.\n",
